<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Archelaus of Cappadocia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/17">17 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Archelaus was the last king of Cappadocia, installed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> in 36 BCE. A loyal client of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, he also received parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> and Lesser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> from Augustus. Summoned to Rome by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> and accused of disloyalty, he died there in 17 CE, and Cappadocia was annexed as a Roman province.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/archelaus-of-cappadocia.jpg" alt="Archelaus of Cappadocia" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins[1] · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archaelaus_of_Cappadocia_-_Drachm.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Archelaus, whose full name was Archelaus Sisines, was a Cappadocian Greek nobleman. He was the first-born son of the Roman client ruler and high priest Archelaus of Comana, who served the goddess Bellona, and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glaphyra">Glaphyra</a>, a hetaera who was famed in antiquity for her beauty. His grandfather, also named Archelaus, was the first of the family to hold the priesthood and rule at Comana and claimed descent from King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a>. In 47 BCE Julius Caesar deposed his father and replaced him with Lycomedes. Glaphyra later became a mistress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Antony deposed and then executed the reigning king, Ariarathes X, in 36 BCE and installed Archelaus. Glaphyra had induced Antony to do so through their affair, and she appeared to hold great power at the royal court. Archelaus supported Antony until Actium in 31 BCE, where he went over to Octavian, and by making peace he kept his crown. Augustus regarded him as loyal. In 25 BCE Augustus gave him Cilicia Trachea, including the harbour city of Elaiussa Sebaste, parts of the Cilician coast and Armenia Minor, which helped suppress piracy and strengthened the border against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>.</p><p>Archelaus moved his palace to Elaiussa Sebaste, built a royal residence and a palace on the island in the harbour, and renamed the city Sebaste in honour of Augustus. He also renamed the village of Garsaura as Archelaïs and made it an administrative centre.</p><p>He wrote a geographical work and a treatise, On Stones and Rivers. He had two children by his first wife, an Armenian princess who died by 8 BCE: a daughter, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glaphyra">Glaphyra</a>, who married Prince Alexander of Judea in 18 or 17 BCE, and a son, Archelaus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>. He acted as a mediator in the Herodian family. In 8 BCE he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythodoris">Pythodorida</a> of Pontus, a union that doubtless Augustus arranged to bind together the royal houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. They had no children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> liked Archelaus, but he had less success with his subjects. Some citizens of Cappadocia accused him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> during Augustus's reign, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>, then at the start of his civil career, successfully defended him. Archelaus later paid more attention to Gaius Caesar, which aroused Tiberius's jealousy. When Tiberius became emperor in 14 CE, Archelaus's health had failed. By 17 CE he had reigned for fifty years.</p><p>That year there was a shortage of funds for military pay, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> wanted to turn Cappadocia into a province. He enticed Archelaus to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where the Senate accused him of plotting a revolution. Archelaus died of natural causes before he could be condemned, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> leaves open that it may have been suicide. Cappadocia became a Roman province, and Armenia Minor went to his stepson Artaxias III.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Archelaus of Cappadocia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The last king, <b>Archelaus</b>, was summoned to Rome by Tiberius and died there in 17 CE, and Cappadocia became a Roman province.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-cappadocia">Kingdom of Cappadocia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Kingdom of Cappadocia. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=7" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Archelaus of Cappadocia"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5788 3947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5788 3947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=7">What was here in 7 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
